===== STATUS: success =====
Question: What methods improve retrieval-augmented generation, including at test time?

Self-RAG improves retrieval-augmented generation through adaptive on-demand retrieval and self-reflection mechanisms (asai2023selfraglearningto pages 1-2). Unlike conventional RAG systems that indiscriminately retrieve a fixed number of passages, Self-RAG trains a language model to generate special reflection tokens that dynamically indicate when retrieval is necessary and evaluate generation quality (asai2023selfraglearningto pages 1-2).

The framework operates through three key token types. Retrieval tokens enable the model to call a retriever on-demand rather than applying fixed retrieval strategies (asai2023selfraglearningto pages 1-2). The model then processes multiple retrieved passages in parallel and generates critique tokens to evaluate and select the best outputs (asai2023selfraglearningto pages 1-2).

At test time, reflection tokens render the model controllable, allowing it to tailor behavior to diverse task requirements (asai2023selfraglearningto pages 1-2). This runtime controllability distinguishes Self-RAG from static retrieval approaches. Additionally, the framework provides citations for each generated segment with self-assessment of whether outputs are supported by retrieved passages, thereby improving fact verification and factuality (asai2023selfraglearningto pages 1-2). This combination of adaptive retrieval, parallel passage processing, quality critique, and runtime controllability collectively enhances RAG performance across varied applications.

